<h1>PHP Password Hashing: Why password_hash Beats MD5</h1>
<p>PHP made password hashing much safer by providing built-in password functions. Older PHP tutorials often used md5() or sha1() for passwords. That advice should not be copied into new code. Modern PHP applications should use password_hash() to create password hashes and password_verify() to check login attempts.</p>
<p>The official <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.password-hash.php">PHP password_hash documentation</a> lists supported algorithms such as bcrypt and Argon2 variants when available. The function returns a complete hash string that includes the algorithm, cost or parameters, salt, and hash data needed for verification. That is much safer than manually combining salts and fast hashes.</p>
<p>A basic signup flow is simple. When the user creates a password, call password_hash with the submitted password and a suitable algorithm constant. Store the returned string in the database. During login, retrieve the stored string and pass it with the submitted password to password_verify. Do not try to parse the string manually unless you have a documented reason.</p>
<p>One benefit of PHP's approach is migration support. password_needs_rehash can tell you when a stored hash should be upgraded because the cost or algorithm changed. That supports gradual security improvements. For example, you can verify an older bcrypt hash during login and then replace it with a stronger hash if the password was correct.</p>
<p>Do not store MD5 password hashes in PHP. MD5 is fast and unsuitable for password storage, as explained in <a href="/why-md5-is-bad-for-passwords.html">Why MD5 Is Bad for Passwords</a>. A salt does not make MD5 equal to bcrypt or Argon2id. It only solves part of the problem and leaves offline guessing too cheap.</p>
<p>Also avoid writing custom password hashing wrappers unless you have strong security review. Common mistakes include reusing salts, using predictable salts, truncating passwords, double-hashing without understanding the result, or losing algorithm metadata. password_hash exists so most applications do not need to design this layer from scratch.</p>
<p>For spring cleanup, review old PHP login code. Search for md5(, sha1(, crypt(, and homemade salt logic. Check password reset scripts as well as normal login files. Many applications update the main login path but forget old admin panels or migration scripts. Those forgotten paths can keep weak hashing alive for years.</p>
<p>Database schema is part of the implementation. Do not store password hashes in a column sized for old MD5 values. Use enough length for current and future algorithms, because password_hash may change defaults over time. Treat the returned string as opaque data and store it exactly as provided by PHP.</p>
<p>For frameworks, check whether the framework already wraps PHP's password functions. Laravel, Symfony, and other systems may provide their own recommended interface. Using the framework's standard method can make configuration, rehashing, and future upgrades easier than calling lower-level functions in scattered files.</p>
<p>Before putting this advice into production, write down the exact use case and risk level. File integrity, password storage, legacy compatibility, and message authentication are different problems. A clear note in the documentation prevents a future developer from copying the same hash choice into a place where it no longer fits.</p>
<p>The practical rule is direct: use password_hash, verify with password_verify, store the full returned string, and use password_needs_rehash for upgrades. For legacy MD5 systems, plan a rehash-on-login migration as described in <a href="/migrate-md5-password-hashes.html">How to Migrate Old MD5 Password Hashes Safely</a>.</p>
